635 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301635 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301635 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186299961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301635 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "80e37fe1-bd7c-51e8-ba48-a531ae7460a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301828 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301828 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301828 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301828 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7f8d121-1dd4-5d54-b226-07216b17964f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd8abfc9-fc42-5fbe-8914-ffe40a38eb8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302137 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1071 "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3518da80-0db9-569e-b980-2b5efc57c5f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302145 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324143 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324283 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324857 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325152 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325152 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325152 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326194 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218632619