36 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492791 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1ef24a4-cbc2-5e7b-841f-6bdc135e149a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x-15$ has a root in the interval $(1,4),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12d33f18-863a-5b25-ace7-f97482164c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492830 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }15}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 15.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $15$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+2x+5).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x+5$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =4-20 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c78b6e5-0b3d-542f-981c-72db0b806463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186302134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492943 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492943 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d4f4a1a-0d09-50c6-9d90-b5ac235d5b30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492943 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492949 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49d109c1-966d-5d06-a1b4-37f9036c2497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186433674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492961 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492961 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7bbb91cb-0115-59be-b84d-383083ba59ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492961 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186335887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492962 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82d25e83-31f3-50b5-8226-00ac5920f5f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492962 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492830 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
